00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.670
Hopefully I'm audible at this point?

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:44.670
You are.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.670
Excellent.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:51.590
OK, well, with that, as Jack said in that
very kind introduction, the group here works

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.880
on microbial
community and human microbiome studies with

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.360
a computational focus.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.229
And this, of course, addresses questions
of how the collection of microbes we carry

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:08.450
in and on our bodies-- bacteria, fungi, viruses,
other types of microbes-

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.659
- affect our health outcomes.

00:02:10.659 --> 00:02:16.590
So I'll to speak today both about some of
the ways in which this affects public health,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.769
and of the accompanying computation and data
science challenges.

00:02:20.769 --> 00:02:25.129
And it's easy to forget after the past few
years worth of interest in the microbiome

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:28.340
that it's actually been studied for
quite a long time.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:34.239
Some of the earliest microscopy done by the
[INAUDIBLE] actually resulted in imaging of

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:37.019
his
own human microbiome, of microbes isolated

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:40.629
from his oral microbial community.

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:42.680
as somebody who sits at a
school of public health, though perhaps some

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.849
of the more urgent reason to study the microbiome
is the rise in

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:53.439
chronic non pathogenic but immunal linked
disease over the past several decades.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:58.909
So collections of diseases like type 1 diabetes
or inflammatory bowel disease-- which I'll

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:04.209
speak about today-- that
are affected by our immune system's interaction

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:07.870
with our typical microbial environment, but
which are not caused

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.510
by a specific pathogen, like an infectious
disease.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:14.449
And the microbiome has now been linked to
a really surprising

00:03:14.449 --> 00:03:17.370
range of chronic disease.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:22.059
Not just gut centered conditions, like inflammatory
bowel disease, but systemic ones like

00:03:22.059 --> 00:03:27.969
type 1 diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, or
even behavioral conditions like autism.

00:03:27.969 --> 00:03:34.079
And it's easy to imagine over the past, say
75 years, the range of environmental exposures

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:37.599
that have changed
how we-- that have changed the interaction

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:40.790
between our immune system and our microbes
that we evolved with

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.010
for millions of preceding years.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.670
It includes some obvious things like diet
or the environment that surrounds us,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:54.099
where we live and work, antimicrobial exposures
that have evolved a great deal since the 1950s.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:58.569
But then also
some less obvious ones, like certainly probiotics,

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:02.390
other pharmaceutical exposures, but even how
we're born, how

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:08.430
we age, or things like our daily stress levels
that can modify how our immune system interacts

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.359
with our natural
microbes.

00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:17.760
So the microbiome provides a high dimensional
readout of human health and of molecular activity

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.930
in much the
same way that, say, human genetics or human

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.880
gene expression can.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.979
This can be used to do things like predict
disease risk or disease prognosis.

00:04:27.979 --> 00:04:31.599
But unlike these other readouts, the microbiome
has the potential to be

00:04:31.599 --> 00:04:32.599
modified.

00:04:32.599 --> 00:04:37.470
So certainly antibiotics are a huge modification,
and there is interest in other types of more

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.530
targeted
modifications of the microbiome to not just

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.940
read out but restore health, once we figure
out the right ways in which

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:45.940
to do it.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.939
So that's one of the main goals of our research.

00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:51.780
And one of the main tools that's used by us
and the entire

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.889
microbiome research community is high throughput
sequencing.

00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:57.280
And, again, this is really aided the past
several

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.330
years renaissance in microbial community studies.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.409
To give a quick overview of how sequencing
is used as one of the molecular tools for

00:05:06.409 --> 00:05:11.879
microbial community
studies, any such study-- be it the human

00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:15.520
microbiome or otherwise-- starts with typically
a culture independent

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:21.310
sample of microbes, and laces the cells--
breaks them open-- to extract nucleotides,

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.630
DNA, or increasingly RNA to
understand microbial transcription as well

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.090
as microbial genomes.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:33.330
There are two common types of sequencing that
are used for microbial communities studies.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:39.400
The first of which I'll refer to is amplicon
sequencing relies on PCR to amplify a single

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.520
marker gene from within this
entire pool of microbial DNA.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.770
This is most commonly regions of the conserved
16S RNA gene.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.900
So this is a gene
that's carried by essentially of all bacteria,

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.380
specifically.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:59.599
And by amplifying only variants of that one
gene, each

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:04.400
variant-- each sequencing read-- can be used
to detect variants at that one position.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.990
So a very cost effective and
efficient way to use sequencing to study microbial

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:10.990
communities.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:15.720
Each read just looks at changes within this
one gene.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.620
And those small changes can be classified
back to

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:23.050
reference databases that tell us where they
came from, which organism they came from.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:28.009
So this gives us
essentially a microbial census in which each

00:06:28.009 --> 00:06:32.550
sample generates a list of microbes and how
many reads were

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.129
classified to them.

00:06:34.129 --> 00:06:39.889
So it counts how much or at what percentage
of each taxon-- each organism-- is detected

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:42.090
per
sample.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:48.169
An alternative to amplicon-- or 16S sequencing
as it's called-- is metagenomic sequencing

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:52.229
of DNA, or meta
transcriptomic sequencing of RNA in which

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:54.979
you just plain sequence everything.

00:06:54.979 --> 00:06:58.889
So very much like shotgun
sequencing of individual genomes-- like the

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:02.340
human genome-- this type of metagenomic or
metatranscriptomic

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.900
sequencing generates a pile of short reads.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.529
But rather than being from a single organism,
they're derived from

00:07:08.529 --> 00:07:12.729
tens or hundreds or sometimes thousands of
different organisms within a community.

00:07:12.729 --> 00:07:17.960
So this can be used to get a more complete
picture of an underlying microbial community

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:22.680
at the cost of some
more computational and data science complexity.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.430
The types of information you can then derive
from this includes

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:31.030
not just quantifying which bugs are there,
but figuring out what genes they might be

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:35.360
carrying, putting-- to some
degree-- those genes back together into microbial

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.740
genomes, figuring out what kinds of metabolism
or

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.599
biochemistry is contained within those genomes.

00:07:41.599 --> 00:07:45.210
We're zooming way in and looking at microbial
genetics and very

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:48.569
precise variants within those microbial communities.

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:50.879
Again, kind of like you would for our human
genetic

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:53.219
population study.

00:07:53.219 --> 00:07:58.689
So these two complementary types of sequencing
are some of the main tools used to generate

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:02.979
data about
microbial communities in the human microbiome.

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:06.340
And they're increasingly accompanied by a
collection of other

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.960
molecular data types that can be derived from
microbiome samples.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.949
And when I say sample here, don't think too
hard about what that might be.

00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:19.560
we'll get into the guts of the situation literally
in a few slides.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.879
Between now and then though, in addition to
sequencing data, microbial communities can

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:29.650
be studied in a number
of ways, briefly summarized here as cellular

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:30.650
data.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.200
So including things like direct visualization
through high

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.620
throughput microscopy, or sometimes culture
based methods-- actually growing organisms

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:42.209
from a sample, rather
than treating each culture independently--

00:08:42.209 --> 00:08:46.360
or using cell sorting methods, such as flow
cytometry, that directly

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.330
counts which cells are there.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:54.380
In addition to these cell focused methods,
other types of biomolecular data-- typically

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.760
derived from microbial
communities-- include mass spec results such

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.870
as metabolomic or proteomic quantification.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:07.610
And these can then
accompany the two types of amplicon or methgenomes

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.180
and methatranscriptomes sequencing described
on the

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.500
previous slide.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:18.250
The types of information that one can get
out of these sequencing data in particular

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.790
include things
like taxonomic profiling.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:24.030
In other words figuring out which bugs are
there like we described for example sequencing.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:25.200
Or there are ways to
In other words figuring out which bugs are

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.200
there like we described for example sequencing.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:30.940
Or there are ways to
zoom in on specific variance within amplicon

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:34.980
to identify not just which organisms, but
which exact strain, which

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.500
exact type of each microbe is present in a
community.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:42.320
These are both possible from amplicon sequencing,
which

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:51.260
again looks at just one gene, one locust or
one source of variation in a microbial community.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.410
Methagenomes and
methatranscriptomes can generate additional

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:57.880
complementary data, such as functional profiling,
which again

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:03.260
quantifies the genes or pathways present in
a collection of communities.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:08.480
Or in addition to taxonomic profiling and
variant haplotyping, like amplicon data can

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.220
generate, methagenomes can provide assemblies.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:16.680
So either whole or
sometimes partial microbial genomes derive

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.589
directly from cultured independent communities.

00:10:20.589 --> 00:10:26.000
And these genomes can also tell you about
which pods, biochemistry, or strain variation

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.000
are present.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.750
So, finally,
from all of these different data types these

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:34.240
can be-- they can be carried downstream for
integration, different types

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:36.840
of statistics, or visualization.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:42.279
And these include things like molecular epidemiology,
associating changes in the

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:48.260
microbiome with covariants-- health outcomes,
disease status, environmental exposures.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:54.839
Further analyzes can be performed focusing
on molecular biology or biochemistry.

00:10:54.839 --> 00:10:58.880
What pathways are microbes
using to generate small molecules that affect

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:03.380
our guts or our systemic health.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.589
Computate-- or comparative
genomics or phylogenetics, so zooming in on

00:11:06.589 --> 00:11:08.380
which strains are present.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:09.760
How they evolve.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.240
How things like
antibiotics might affect a microbial selection

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:14.509
in the entire microbiome.

00:11:14.509 --> 00:11:19.529
Or, finally, things like systems biology or
biophysical models, looking at the dynamics

00:11:19.529 --> 00:11:22.860
of community change using combinations of
these data.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:29.380
So I'll touch on several of these different
data analysis types from multiple different

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:33.980
types of microbiome data
today, using mainly examples from the Human

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:35.480
Microbiome Project.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:40.839
This will include both what is commonly
referred to as the Human Microbiome Project

00:11:40.839 --> 00:11:46.709
or HMP now-- which I'll refer to as HMP1,
having wrapped up a few

00:11:46.709 --> 00:11:47.940
years ago now.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:56.160
So the HMP1 was an NAH funded project to define
a baseline of normal human microbial

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.100
variation.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:03.470
Unlike our genomes-- which are 99.9% plus
similar from person to person, and the last

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:07.910
few variants obviously
matter quite a bit-- our microbiomes rarely

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:11.350
overlap by more than 10% from person to person.

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:15.580
There's a huge
amount of normal baseline variation in what

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:16.990
microbes we carry.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:21.399
So several years ago now, the HMP1 set out
in a

00:12:21.399 --> 00:12:28.690
quote, healthy or baseline population, to
define the bounds of this normal variation.

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:32.300
Or really to just understand
who's there, which microbes are present in

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:36.959
what configuration in the absence of perturbations
from disease.

00:12:36.959 --> 00:12:44.980
So after wrapping up in 2012, the HMP1 provided
sort of a baseline catalog to contextualize

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:48.899
disease associated
variations in the microbiology.

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:54.470
Those were further study by the HMP2-- or
integrative HMP-- which is just recently

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:55.470
wrapping up.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:13:01.680
And this has more of a functional mandate
from the NIH to analyze the biology, and specifically

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:07.329
functional molecular biology, of both the
microbiome and close interaction dynamically

00:13:07.329 --> 00:13:11.459
over time, and with a focus
on disease in addition to this quote, healthy

00:13:11.459 --> 00:13:13.560
baseline.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:19.399
So the HMP2 consisted of three main projects
with three specific disease targets.

00:13:19.399 --> 00:13:23.170
I'll speak today about our work
in inflammatory bowel disease.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:27.060
And this project was accompanied by two other
than the HMP2 on pregnancy and

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:31.570
pre-term birth at Virginia Commonwealth, and
type 2 diabetes and obesity at Stanford and

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:32.570
Jackson labs.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:37.819
As you'll see in several slides, all of these
project's data are available through our Data

00:13:37.819 --> 00:13:41.170
Coordination Center run at
the University of Maryland.

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:47.220
So to speak a little bit to inflammatory bowel
disease as an environment in which to

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:53.370
study the microbiome, it's been a model for
different types of gut microbial dysbiosis

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:56.790
for a surprisingly long period
of time.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:02.060
So even before the recent burst of work in
human microbiome studies, you can go back

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:07.879
many decades
and find language in publications that's surprisingly

00:14:07.879 --> 00:14:12.079
modern, with respect to describing how the
gut microbiome is

00:14:12.079 --> 00:14:17.750
perturbed in a complex way in inflammatory
bowel disease.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:23.560
And so IBD became a model for microbiome studies
over the course, again, of several decades.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.980
Starting in the
1940s and 1950s when the disease was observed

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:30.920
to be sometimes responsive to antibiotic treatment,
but without

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:36.269
a single isolatable or cultural pathogen that
seemed to be causative.

00:14:36.269 --> 00:14:40.579
This continued over the subsequent few
decades, as other types of treatments were

00:14:40.579 --> 00:14:46.230
introduced-- [INAUDIBLE] that are still common
treatments for

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:51.990
inflammatory bowel diseases, which affect
microbes without being exquisitely anti-microbial.

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:56.770
But also without
targeting a single causative pathogen.

00:14:56.770 --> 00:15:02.259
And then, finally, in the 1970s and 80s--
as genetically modified mice were introduced--

00:15:02.259 --> 00:15:07.540
several of them
recapitulate IBD like phenotypes with a disruptive

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:08.540
microbiome.

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:10.709
So, again, without a single organism that
could be

00:15:10.709 --> 00:15:14.140
blamed for this disease.

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:17.480
And then in the past decade or so as sequencing
has become such a common tool to

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:23.089
better understand how the gut microbiome is
disrupted in IBD, things progressed fairly

00:15:23.089 --> 00:15:24.089
rapidly.

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:27.360
From early
observations of reduced diversity or dysbiosis--

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:32.139
so just lacking the typical range of microbes
found in the gut--

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:37.089
through identifying which organisms become
more or less abundant during IBD.

00:15:37.089 --> 00:15:42.279
To finally starting to differentiate
how these affect the main subsets of inflammatory

00:15:42.279 --> 00:15:43.279
bowel disease.

00:15:43.279 --> 00:15:46.519
So these include things like ulcerative colitis,
Crohn's disease.

00:15:46.519 --> 00:15:47.550
And you'll see some more details about these
on

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.800
the next two slides.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:55.850
So in terms of how these data can be analyzed,
you can, again, compare a lot about these

00:15:55.850 --> 00:16:00.759
sort of complex disruptions in the microbiome
to complex human genetics.

00:16:00.759 --> 00:16:06.790
So if you think about infectious disease or
Mendelian human genetic diseases, these are

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:09.450
both due to a single
cause of large effect.

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:13.629
So a genetic disorder like cystic fibrosis
really comes from a single locus, and that

00:16:13.629 --> 00:16:16.129
causes
most of the disease effect.

00:16:16.129 --> 00:16:21.120
From a microbial side, a parallel would be
a pathogen.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.589
So something like color that's
really caused by a single organism of large

00:16:24.589 --> 00:16:25.589
effect.

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:30.370
There's been a great deal of work on complex
human genetics in the past two decades.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:34.190
And these include traits
and diseases that are caused, not by one to

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:37.970
locus of large effect, but by the synergistic
effect of many genes or

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:40.240
pathways working together.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:44.610
And this seems to be the case in complex microbial
dysbiosis as well, in which there's

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:50.319
not a single pathogen but a functional consequence
of many bugs, or the normal ecology of the

00:16:50.319 --> 00:16:55.350
gut or other parts
of the human microbiome becoming disrupted.

00:16:55.350 --> 00:17:01.579
So to better understanding of disruption in
inflammatory bowel disease, during HMP2, we

00:17:01.579 --> 00:17:04.680
worked with Ramnik
Xavier's group at [INAUDIBLE] to put together

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:09.450
a cohort of just over 100 individuals who
were followed for one year

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:10.450
each.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:15.480
Consisting about half of Crohn's disease patients,
a quarter of ulcerative colitis patients,

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:17.080
and about a quarter
non-idd controls.

00:17:17.080 --> 00:17:23.040
The cohort was recruited from a series of
different clinical centers, roughly half adults

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.780
and half
pediatrics.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:31.540
And these very generous patients contributed
stool samples every two years-- or sorry--

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:33.180
every two
weeks for one year.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:39.530
These were our primary sample type with which
to study the gut microbiome, in addition to

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:43.100
colon biopsies
collected at enrollment, and blood draws collected

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:44.730
roughly quarterly.

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:50.020
So from these three different sample types,
we are able to get a whole set of different

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:51.420
data.

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:57.780
Much of the stool derived data described the
microbiome, mainly

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:03.310
using sequencing, metagenomics and metatranscriptomics,
in addition to a series of other molecular

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:06.380
data types,
metabolomics and proteomics from mass spec--

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:11.810
like I mentioned earlier-- a subset of virally
targeted sequencing.

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:15.900
And then some of hos targeted measurements
from the stool samples, including [INAUDIBLE],

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:20.220
which is a protein
measure of gut inflammation.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:26.470
Our biopsies and blood draws provided mostly
human focused data, some of which was, again,

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:27.470
sequencing.

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:33.690
Including exome sequencing of the human hosts,
epigenetic [INAUDIBLE] sequencing of methylation--

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:37.010
both right
in the gut from the biopsies and in circulating

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:38.010
blood.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:41.330
And then a couple of other non sequencing
data types there,

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:46.050
including serology from the blood-- so antibody
markers in circulating blood.

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:50.920
And, finally, 16S or amplicon
sequencing of microbes, again, directly in

00:18:50.920 --> 00:18:53.140
the gut from these biopsy samples.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:57.160
So you can see from these numbers down the
side that there are several thousand samples

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:01.490
touching many
different data types describing, as mandated,

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:07.870
both microbial and host changes over time
during IBD.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:11.170
To give an
overview of how this broke down over both

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:17.350
time and the individuals within the populations,
for each phenotype--

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:21.850
Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, and our
control individuals-- we had a sub-population

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:23.950
of it very densely
sampled.

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:29.570
So generating data from almost every sample
provided over the course of the year.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:33.480
And then subpopulations
that were targeted to specific aligned time

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.920
points.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:41.120
What this allowed us to do is generate multi
omic data to integrate as many of these different

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.900
data types as
possible over time.

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:50.690
So this complicated subway diagram here is
essentially a venn diagram listing the overlap

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:54.790
between these different data types, anchored
off of methagenomics sequencing, for which

00:19:54.790 --> 00:19:57.530
we had a total of just
under 1,700 samples.

00:19:57.530 --> 00:20:03.520
And most of these overlapped with one or more
other molecular data types.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:08.320
So I won't go through that-- the details here,
but for example, essentially all of the methatranscriptome

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:12.660
sequence
data sets were drawn from the same samples

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:14.720
as a methagenome.

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:22.210
Essentially all of the methaproteinomic and
metabolomic samples were also drawn from underlying

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.740
methagenomes, and so forth, and so forth.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:28.310
Down to
roughly 300 samples, for which we have all

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:33.540
six of these microbially targeted data types
from the same sample

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:34.790
time points and individuals.

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:40.380
So this gives us an opportunity to look at
how each measurement of the microbiome changes

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:45.240
over time, and how
they integrate with each other cross-sectionally

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:46.850
and dynamically.

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:50.540
So all of these data are being made available
as

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:51.740
they are generated.

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:58.040
Essentially all of them are online currently
at the IBD multiomic database or IBD MDV portal,

00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:02.620
in addition to the main HMP Data Coordinating
Center that I mentioned earlier.

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:07.450
The IBD MDV has a little more
focused information for our project in particular,

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:10.410
including all of the protocols that we used
for sample collection

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.550
and data generation.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:15.970
So if you're interested in doing your own
study and really want to collect thousands

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:19.900
of stool samples, there's a
protocol describing how we did that using

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:24.550
a home based kit that could be dropped in
the mail and shipped in.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:27.100
And
then, of course, all the raw and processed

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:32.270
data are being made available on the IBD MDV
as well, along with the

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:38.100
clinical metadata describing these patients,
their medical histories, their medication,

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:41.850
and how these change over
the course of a year.

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:45.630
To show a few first looks at how we've been
integrating some of these different molecular

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:52.430
data types in the HM2, I'll show an example
first of work comparing the gut metagenome

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:54.490
to the get
metatranscriptome.

00:21:54.490 --> 00:22:00.160
So in other words, the collection of DNA that's
present in microbes during IBD, and the

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:03.760
collection of RNA-- or their transcriptional
activity-- in the gut.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:11.290
So this is an example from one pathway, N-acetygucosamine
degradation, which is a type of mucous metabolism

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.960
carried out by a subset of gut microbes.

00:22:14.960 --> 00:22:20.230
Each column here represents the distribution
of which organisms or

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:25.670
expressing, transcriptionally, this pathway
across a subset of one of our pilot data sets

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:26.670
within the HMP2.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:29.150
So
there's a smaller number of samples here,

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:30.150
obviously.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:35.770
In almost every individual, bacteroides vulgatus--
this specific species that's common in the

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:40.260
gut-- is uniquely
responsible for this type of mucous metabolism.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:42.960
And when we look at this not just cross-sectionally
but

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:47.650
longitudinally that's true within most subjects
over time as well.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:50.800
So when we take one patient's four time points
that

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:59.060
we have in this pilot data set, at each time
point, bacteroides vulgatus is both the only

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:04.090
organism carrying this
pathway in its genome, and the only organism

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:06.290
transcribe-- essentially the only organism
transcribing it.

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:09.790
So this is
likely the bug responsible for this particular

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:13.750
portion of mucous metabolism in the gut for
this individual.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:15.860
But that's not true for all patients.

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:20.480
So if we go to another individual with more
active disease, bacteroides vulgatus

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:25.820
is typically the dominant organism carrying
this pathway in its DNA.

00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:30.290
But other organisms take over that
functionality at different points in time.

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:36.030
So even very rare organisms can become responsible
for this type of

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.640
biochemistry transiently.

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:42.970
So in this second time point, a different
bacteroides, the [INAUDIBLE] has taken over

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:45.870
[INAUDIBLE] of the pathway.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:50.210
In the third time point, bacteroides massiliensis
has taken over, and so forth.

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:55.800
As this patient goes through more
active changes in their [INAUDIBLE] status,

00:23:55.800 --> 00:24:01.200
the organisms carrying this pathway don't
change.

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.260
But the organism is
carrying out this activity.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:09.540
The organism transcribing this particular
mucous metabolism pathway change from time

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:11.040
point of time point.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:17.310
So this can reflect an aspect of instability
in the gut microbiome corresponding to disease

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:20.580
activity that's not
necessarily visible in just measuring the

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:24.550
DNA, but which can be seen in which bugs are
carrying out a pathway

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:29.140
transcriptome, This seems to be a general
principle within the gut.

00:24:29.140 --> 00:24:31.330
Not just true in disease activity.

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:36.510
But even a
baseline if we compare which organisms can

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:41.660
contribute all pathways to which organisms
are actively transcribing

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:42.660
them.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:46.760
So here on the y-axis each point represents
one pathway.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:50.730
Just like this overall path does boiled down
to a single

00:24:50.730 --> 00:24:51.730
point.

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:58.300
On the y-axis is the diversity of how many
organisms can contribute a pathway.

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:02.290
On the x-axis is the diversity
of how many organisms are transcribing that

00:25:02.290 --> 00:25:04.110
pathway.

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:06.400
Everything is above the diagonal, which is
good.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.790
You expect more bugs to carry pathway than
to activate it at any

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:11.330
given time.

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:13.820
Excuse me.

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:18.270
And what's interesting here is that in the
lower left are the more unique pathways, those

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:20.390
that are only carried by a few bugs.

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:24.720
And if you look at the coloring, this corresponds
to how actively transcribed

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:25.720
that pathway is.

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:27.720
And these tend to light up.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:33.220
So the more unique a functionality is, the
more it tends to be over-expressed.

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:36.150
Another example of this in a
different organism is methanagenesis.

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:42.340
This is a pathway that's really only present
in a very few bugs, the archaea

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:43.380
in the gut.

00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:47.590
And it can be over expressed by as much as
100-fold.

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:53.240
So those few bugs that can perform that specific
biochemistry, tend to work really hard at

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:54.240
it.

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:56.100
They're carrying out their particular function,
their particular niche in

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:58.140
the gut.

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:04.150
We can perform this type of multiomic data
comparison for other combinations of molecular

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:07.090
data in the
microbiome as well.

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:11.620
So that example compared methagenomes to methatranscriptomes
in one of our pilot data

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:12.620
sets.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:18.440
We can do something similar comparing methagenomes
to metabolomes, or the abundances of the different

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:24.120
small molecules in the gut in the stool samples
from the HMP2.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:28.170
So this is a slightly different pilot data
set, again, not including the stool final

00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:29.770
sample set.

00:26:29.770 --> 00:26:32.950
But here we've
measured-- in addition to methagenomes-- the

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:36.500
abundances of about 400 or 500 identified
compounds, and

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:44.861
several thousand unidentified compounds in
collaboration with [INAUDIBLE] group at the

00:26:44.861 --> 00:26:45.861
Broad Institute.

00:26:45.861 --> 00:26:48.280
So this
plot summarizes the classes and number of

00:26:48.280 --> 00:26:53.160
each class of compounds that are individually
enriched or depleted in

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:58.490
Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis, very
much like the microbial enrichments or depletions

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:01.570
in IBD that I
mentioned earlier.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:06.960
But where things get more interesting is when
we start integrating these small molecules

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:10.360
chemical changes with
the microbial changes.

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:20.720
This plot shows the correlation of organisms
that are either enriched or depleted during

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:26.440
inflammatory bowel disease with small molecules,
chemicals, that are enriched in the gut or

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:28.790
depleted during
inflammatory bowel disease.

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:33.750
So each cell in the heat mass correlates a
bug with a compound.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:38.080
And this is after
correcting for a variety of co-variants--

00:27:38.080 --> 00:27:43.180
so things like subject age, antibiotic exposure,
or even disease activity

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:44.780
itself.

00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:48.780
So even though these are bugs and compounds
that are disrupted in disease, we tried to

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:52.171
regress out as much of
that effect as we can so we can instead look

00:27:52.171 --> 00:27:57.120
at microbial biochemical co-variation.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:28:01.180
What this then means when a
bug-- or what it suggests when a bug in a

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:05.280
small molecule co-variant exists strongly
across the population, is that

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:07.600
there might be something mechanistic going
on.

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:11.970
This could be a compound that a positively
correlated organism

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:17.020
generates biochemically, or it could be a
compound that co-varies because of this bug

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:19.780
depends on it as a nutrient.

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:26.460
Or alternatively, an organism might ecologically
interact with another organism that generates

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.530
or depends on this
compound.

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:33.780
And some of these co-variations are so strong.

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:38.250
We find a lot of very striking correlations
between microbes and

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:39.250
very specific compounds.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:44.190
Some of these are so strong that we can do
some gazing into the methagenomes to

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:51.180
help explain mechanistically why these observational
correlations might be happening.

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:55.190
An example of that-- we'll see an example
of that from ruminococcus gnavus, specifically

00:28:55.190 --> 00:28:57.510
in a few slides.

00:28:57.510 --> 00:29:01.030
But it's
surprising how often these correlations happen

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:03.400
for unintuitive reasons.

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:06.500
There's a lot of-- a theme that I'll come
back to a couple of times, that there's a

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:11.200
lot we don't know about microbial biochemistry
in the wild, in the gut, and

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.250
in other parts of the human microbiome.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:21.660
So when we go looking for known enzymes--
annotated microbial genes that are non degenerate

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:26.030
or metabolize
specific compounds-- we find very few cases

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:30.540
in which we can correlate a known enzyme with
the compound that it

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:33.130
generates or consumes.

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:36.540
There are a few of these examples with ethanolamine
or glutatione, for example.

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:40.060
And
these are annotations of the enzymes responsible

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:43.910
for processing those compounds across the
samples, and in a

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:45.580
variety of different organisms.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:49.180
But these are the exceptions rather than the
rule.

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:51.930
And if there are microbiologists in the audience
and you look

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:56.490
closely at this slide, you can actually guess
why this might be happening, in as much as

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:58.540
there's a lot of green.

00:29:58.540 --> 00:30:01.870
So
the color here indicates which organisms are

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:06.740
contributing these enzymes in each of the
three genotypes, IBD,

00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:11.600
Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis and controls.

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:17.110
And if you compare the enrichments for these
organisms to the legend below, you can see

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:21.050
that green is actually
e.coli, which is one of the best annotated

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:24.780
organisms were we do know quite a bit of biochemistry,
and we can

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:28.720
assign biochemical function to many of its
genes.

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:32.800
But there are very few cases where we can
find this specific of

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:34.110
information so far.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:39.350
So even though we can identify genes that
co-vary with compounds, they may be completely

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:40.350
unannotated.

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:43.910
So there's a lot of biochemistry left to discover
there.

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:48.600
We can-- just from a purely computational
or machine learning perspective-- use those

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:52.710
unannotated genes or
compounds to ask questions about IBD more

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:53.710
broadly.

00:30:53.710 --> 00:30:59.950
So, for example, it's fairly easy to fairly
well differentiate the

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:06.710
IBD gut from a controlled microbiome-- so
from non IBD or quote healthy controls.

00:31:06.710 --> 00:31:09.840
So using, in this case, a
random forest classifier-- but this is true

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:14.640
for essentially any descriminative of machine
learning algorithm-- we can

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:21.520
separate individuals with and without IBD
with fairly good accuracy using either a measurement

00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:24.270
of which
chemicals are there, a measurement of which

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:27.370
bugs are there, or it's not on this plot--
we can do the same thing

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:30.810
using a measure of which genes are there.

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:35.380
The classification test gets a little bit
harder when we differentiate the sub-types

00:31:35.380 --> 00:31:36.380
of IBD.

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:39.420
So, for example,
separating ulcerative colitis from Crohn's

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:41.800
disease drops the accuracy a little bit.

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:46.450
But what I think is interesting
about this result was that the machine learner

00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:52.070
can do roughly as well whether it's learning
from chemicals or which

00:31:52.070 --> 00:31:53.560
organisms are present in the microbiome.

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:58.160
Or, again, not shown here, but the same is
true for genes and pathways in the microbiome.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:02.370
Which, again, points
to the very tight link between which bugs

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:06.480
and which biochemical DNA is present-- which
enzymes are present in

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:11.670
the gut-- and the metabolite pool that we
observed through your metabolic profiling

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:12.670
of the gut microbiome.

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:19.380
So, finally, to get to one more specific example
of this-- by zooming further into that ruminococcus

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:22.650
gnavus
example I mentioned earlier-- we have a third

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:27.750
pilot data set from within the HMP-- within
the HMP2-- that I'll use

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:35.130
for this example, which compared specifically
the dense, longer, longitudinal time forces

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:40.570
across Crohn's disease
and a subset of the control subjects.

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:47.190
So in taking a look at this longitudinal data
set, Branley Miranda, the post doc

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:53.510
who worked on this project did, was ask questions
about the functionality of microbes that were

00:32:53.510 --> 00:32:59.380
changing in IBD,
or in Crohn's disease specifically over time.

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:04.360
And one of the things that they found that's
been suggested by metagenomic profiling earlier

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:07.950
is that there are
more commonalities in the types of biochemistry

00:33:07.950 --> 00:33:12.360
that gets perturbed during IBD than in the
specific organisms.

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:16.065
And one example of this is the class of facilitative
anaerobes, or aero tolerant microbes in the

00:33:16.065 --> 00:33:17.065
gut, tend to be
And one example of this is the class of facilitative

00:33:17.065 --> 00:33:20.210
anaerobes, or aero tolerant microbes in the
gut, tend to be

00:33:20.210 --> 00:33:21.880
enriched in IBD.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:24.820
And the causality for this could go either
way.

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:30.690
So this could either be a function of more
oxygen being present in the gut during inflammation

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.760
and allowing these
bugs to grow.

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:38.310
Or these bugs being pro-inflammatory and sort
of causing the inflammation and the IBD in

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:39.310
the first
place.

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:44.470
But you can see this in enrichment for a whole
group of different organisms either when comparing

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:47.500
the
Crohn's and control individuals from within

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:53.010
this HMP2 pilot data set, or when comparing
it to other external got

00:33:53.010 --> 00:33:59.070
microbiome data sets, including the healthy
population in the HMP1, or another Crohn's

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:04.640
disease study carried out
at University of Pennsylvania.

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:09.230
Individual organisms may not show such a strong
enrichment during inflammation.

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:12.970
But the entire class of aero
tolerant organisms does.

00:34:12.970 --> 00:34:18.080
And you can see this when we zoom into ruminococcus
gnavus as a representative of

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:19.409
this group of organisms.

00:34:19.409 --> 00:34:25.669
It's weakly enriched individually, but the
overall functional enrichment is a lot more

00:34:25.669 --> 00:34:28.270
statistically significant.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:32.869
What's interesting about ruminococcus gnavus
though, is that one of the reasons it's so

00:34:32.869 --> 00:34:36.970
hard to see this
enrichment in an individual organism is that

00:34:36.970 --> 00:34:41.079
it's not the whole organism that seems to
be associated with

00:34:41.079 --> 00:34:44.440
inflammatory bowel disease and inflammation
of the microbiome.

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:49.079
It's only certain strains of ruminococcus
gnavus.

00:34:49.079 --> 00:34:54.899
So what you're looking at here is on each
column, is a one sample from either a Crohn's

00:34:54.899 --> 00:34:59.349
disease or control
individual who did carry ruminococcus gnavus

00:34:59.349 --> 00:35:00.349
in their gut.

00:35:00.349 --> 00:35:02.950
And you can see there's a small number of
control

00:35:02.950 --> 00:35:06.769
individuals for carrying ruminococcus gnavus.

00:35:06.769 --> 00:35:12.210
But there are about 200 genes-- and that what
each row represents-- that were only carried

00:35:12.210 --> 00:35:14.920
by the strains of
microbes-- that strains-- of ruminococcus

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:17.830
gnavus present in the IBD gut.

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:22.569
And we can put biochemical annotations
on a small set of these genes.

00:35:22.569 --> 00:35:27.029
Some of them deal with aero tolerance and
oxidative stress management.

00:35:27.029 --> 00:35:29.660
Others
correspond to more pathogen or pathobiont-like

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:32.559
functions like adhesion or invasion.

00:35:32.559 --> 00:35:36.319
And others have really
interesting metabolic annotations like iron

00:35:36.319 --> 00:35:38.009
utilization.

00:35:38.009 --> 00:35:40.579
But the vast majority are, again, unannotates.

00:35:40.579 --> 00:35:49.349
They don't have good homology to other well-characterized
enzymes out in reference databases.

00:35:49.349 --> 00:35:53.779
So in as much as I'll, again, come back to
this a few times, it's a

00:35:53.779 --> 00:35:58.170
microbiological or biochemical challenge to
figure out what these genes do.

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:03.230
But I as a computational biologist, I
would also frame it as a data management challenge.

00:36:03.230 --> 00:36:07.030
So a lot of low hanging fruit in using slightly
more intelligent

00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:12.440
algorithms than just plain homology to start
getting an idea of what some of this microbial

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:16.039
dark matter that seems
functionally significant in the microbiome

00:36:16.039 --> 00:36:17.039
might be doing.

00:36:17.039 --> 00:36:23.090
So with that in mind, I'll spend some of the
rest of the time on what you can think of

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:29.319
as the other HMP2, or a
second wave of analysis carried out in the

00:36:29.319 --> 00:36:33.020
quote healthy or baseline population of the
HMP1.

00:36:33.020 --> 00:36:36.720
So to help
differentiate this population, I'll refer

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:39.880
to examples from this study as the HMP1-II.

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:46.829
so this is a set of similarly
flavored analyzes carried out on methagenomes

00:36:46.829 --> 00:36:50.650
from the HMP1 healthy population.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:55.140
And so I introduced this briefly earlier--
but to go into a little bit more detail of

00:36:55.140 --> 00:37:02.039
how the HMP1 defined a baseline
microbiome-- this population included 300

00:37:02.039 --> 00:37:06.579
quote healthy subjects who were screened at
five different body areas

00:37:06.579 --> 00:37:11.019
for a fairly high and specific degree of clinical
health.

00:37:11.019 --> 00:37:14.911
So very few oral health or oral hygiene issues.

00:37:14.911 --> 00:37:18.510
There was a
specific BMI range that had to be followed.

00:37:18.510 --> 00:37:21.859
No skin abrasions, for example.

00:37:21.859 --> 00:37:24.760
And this allowed up to 18 different bodies
sites to be sampled.

00:37:24.760 --> 00:37:28.380
Not just the gut, but a series of nasal.

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:31.089
Many
different oral sites across the mouth.

00:37:31.089 --> 00:37:32.529
A few different skin sites.

00:37:32.529 --> 00:37:34.619
Again, stool representing the gut, and a series
of

00:37:34.619 --> 00:37:38.339
different vaginal microbiome samples in women.

00:37:38.339 --> 00:37:43.900
The HMP1 and 1-II included three different
visits to get a little bit of longitudinal

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:47.480
information for this population as
well.

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:53.070
And as well as, again, metadata describing
the demographics and background and environment

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:55.289
of these
individuals.

00:37:55.289 --> 00:38:01.019
So the initial sets of information published
with the HMP1 several years ago focused mainly

00:38:01.019 --> 00:38:05.119
on the
several amplicon samples that were analyzed

00:38:05.119 --> 00:38:08.910
across essentially all of these samples that
were collected.

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:17.470
For the HMP1-II, more recently we just finished
analysis of about 2,400 methagenomes across

00:38:17.470 --> 00:38:21.760
a subset of these
individual and body sites, but now spanning

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:26.569
roughly all three time points for all individuals,
and a subset of six

00:38:26.569 --> 00:38:29.240
representative body sites from the different
areas.

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:31.900
So we'll talk about some of the results from
that.

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:35.579
I think some of
the interesting data challenges here was certainly

00:38:35.579 --> 00:38:39.660
wrangling this large a set of methagenomes,
performing things

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:46.380
like quality control, raw bioinformatics on
the methagenomic sequencing data.

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:51.549
And methagenomic assembly was
greatly facilitated by, again, Owen White's

00:38:51.549 --> 00:38:56.940
team at the Data Coordinating Center, who
are doing this as part of the

00:38:56.940 --> 00:39:01.420
NIH Data Commons Project, in addition to the
HMP2.

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:06.391
What we could then get out of that is not
just more data than we had in the HMP1, but

00:39:06.391 --> 00:39:10.080
a series of updated
analyzes, including very specific functional

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.460
profiling-- that we'll see in a few slides.

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:16.009
Very specific taxonomic
profiling-- down to the tracking of individual

00:39:16.009 --> 00:39:17.009
strains.

00:39:17.009 --> 00:39:19.200
And we'll describe some of the computing behind
that as well.

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:24.799
And then a few things I won't have time for
today, including non-bacterial profiling--

00:39:24.799 --> 00:39:30.500
fungi and viruses-- the
updated assembly that I mentioned, gene catalogues

00:39:30.500 --> 00:39:32.650
that derived from these assemblies, and then
a little bit

00:39:32.650 --> 00:39:37.060
about the multiple time point dynamics that
I will describe the analysis methods for.

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:43.380
But to start with some of the easy views of
what this information can tell you, to start

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:48.400
at a 30,000 foot view of the
overall human microbiome.

00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:52.520
These methagenomic profiles now show much
the same information that was

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:58.440
available from the amplicon data originally
in the HMP1, in which many of the largest

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:02.960
differences in the human
microbiome across the body are derived from

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:04.490
the body site itself.

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:08.240
Bug care about their immediate biochemical
environments.

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:10.280
Microbes are essentially little bags of biochemistry.

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:15.200
So when you ask, well, what are the biggest
differences across the human microbiome, the

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:19.769
first answer is the
immediate environment, the body site that

00:40:19.769 --> 00:40:23.200
a collection of microbes likes to live in.

00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:27.180
So that means when we put
these 2,400 metagenomes together, most of

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:31.900
the variation that we can see at a high level
derives from the

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:34.700
different body sites from which those samples
are derived.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:37.130
The gut is a very unique environment that
tends to

00:40:37.130 --> 00:40:40.460
carry out a different microbial ecology.

00:40:40.460 --> 00:40:42.859
The same is true for skin.

00:40:42.859 --> 00:40:46.971
The vaginal microbiome sights were quite similar
to each other, but distinct from the

00:40:46.971 --> 00:40:49.740
other microbial environment samples here.

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:52.500
And then I mentioned there were multiple different
oral body sites,

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:57.069
which you can see here in the kind of cool
colors, and a continuum in which we can actually--

00:40:57.069 --> 00:41:00.299
I'll shows some
examples later-- differentiate fairly specific

00:41:00.299 --> 00:41:05.710
locations within the oral cavity, such as
the tooth surface, the cheek, or

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:08.920
the tongue, which were the three main oral
sights studied here.

00:41:08.920 --> 00:41:15.119
This is a very high level view, in which each
point represents a sample, and the distance

00:41:15.119 --> 00:41:17.670
between points
represents how dissimilar they are.

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:22.279
But, of, course this is supported by very
detailed information underneath--

00:41:22.279 --> 00:41:27.640
both per sample listing which organisms are
there and their abundances-- and as we'll

00:41:27.640 --> 00:41:31.999
see in a few slides,
differentiating those organisms into specific

00:41:31.999 --> 00:41:35.180
strains as well.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:40.480
The first analysis I want to described using
the information about these taxonomic profiles

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:44.279
though, was in
characterizing how they changed over time.

00:41:44.279 --> 00:41:49.400
This is something new within the HMP1-II,
and uses what I think was

00:41:49.400 --> 00:41:55.450
an interesting data analysis method for this
type of data, specifically Gaussian processes.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:42:01.220
So you can think of a
Gaussian process as a probabilistic functional

00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:02.220
regression.

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:05.749
So a way of fitting a curve probabilistically
to a series of

00:42:05.749 --> 00:42:07.070
measurements over time.

00:42:07.070 --> 00:42:09.050
Which is exactly what we have here.

00:42:09.050 --> 00:42:14.480
We have a series of multiple time points measured
for the microbiome, each

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:19.520
measuring a collection of organisms in different
individuals, and in different 46 body sets.

00:42:19.520 --> 00:42:25.609
So we wanted to ask how different organisms
were changing over time across the body within

00:42:25.609 --> 00:42:26.999
the microbiome.

00:42:26.999 --> 00:42:32.549
And Gaussian processes gave us a way to do
that by decomposing each of these set of changes

00:42:32.549 --> 00:42:34.490
into a couple of
specific mechanisms.

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:40.680
So specifically, we built a set of Gaussian
processes to model microbial change as a

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:44.079
combination of stable inter-individual differences.

00:42:44.079 --> 00:42:48.079
So bugs that are just all the difference between
people, and their

00:42:48.079 --> 00:42:52.230
abundances depend on who you are, not what
time it is.

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:55.410
Microbes that very steadily over time.

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:58.720
And these are organisms that might, for example,
be responsive to long

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:04.980
term dietary perturbations, or long term medical
pharmaceutical interventions.

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:07.079
So things like antibiotics.

00:43:07.079 --> 00:43:11.799
And then, finally, since we had a series of
technical replicators, we could to differentiate

00:43:11.799 --> 00:43:16.410
between microbes that
change rapidly on their own biologically versus

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:19.509
those that have measurement error and have
essentially technical

00:43:19.509 --> 00:43:20.509
noise.

00:43:20.509 --> 00:43:26.119
So you can think of this as rapid change,
and this as a measurement error.

00:43:26.119 --> 00:43:33.589
So by fitting probabilistic this
hierarchical Gaussian process to our longitudinal

00:43:33.589 --> 00:43:37.380
data, we can describe each bug in terms of
these three

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:38.750
properties of interest.

00:43:38.750 --> 00:43:41.640
So this differs ably between individuals.

00:43:41.640 --> 00:43:46.660
Does it change slowly over time, or does it
change rapidly over time?

00:43:46.660 --> 00:43:54.130
So these three locations in the plot I'm about
to show correspond to these three temporal

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:55.130
patterns.

00:43:55.130 --> 00:43:59.799
State-- the stability between individuals,
slow temporal

00:43:59.799 --> 00:44:01.940
change, and rapid temporal change.

00:44:01.940 --> 00:44:07.880
And we can then plot over this complex each
organism.

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:09.960
Or it turns out-- and I won't show it today--
you can do the

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:13.500
same thing for pathways within a particular
body site.

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:16.990
So it's here we've broken down the gut microbiome
in that

00:44:16.990 --> 00:44:22.480
healthy population of the HMP1-II based on
these temporal behaviors.

00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:26.400
And you can immediately see some
consistency in what the different microbes

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:27.630
are doing.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:33.400
The bacteroides, which are one of the dominant
groups of bugs in the gut, tend to be stable

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:35.200
within individuals over
time.

00:44:35.200 --> 00:44:38.829
Some people will have one bacteroides, and
some people have a different type of bacteroides.

00:44:38.829 --> 00:44:44.980
These blue dots represent another major group
of organisms in the gut, the fermicutes.

00:44:44.980 --> 00:44:48.589
They tend overall to show
more slow variation.

00:44:48.589 --> 00:44:52.580
So this might include things like diet responsiveness.

00:44:52.580 --> 00:44:57.829
And then, finally, a subset of more opportunistic
organisms like e.coli change rapidly over

00:44:57.829 --> 00:44:58.829
time.

00:44:58.829 --> 00:45:02.490
They can grow or
die off or grow again very quickly in response

00:45:02.490 --> 00:45:04.599
to a changing environment.

00:45:04.599 --> 00:45:11.510
So zooming in to a slightly higher level
of taxonomic information, we can profile not

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:15.280
just which organisms are present in the gut
microbiome, but which

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:18.380
strains of each organism are present.

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:24.509
And one-- a quick one slide summary of one
of the computational methods for doing this

00:45:24.509 --> 00:45:30.240
relies on mapping the
short reads within a metagenome to a collection

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:36.250
of pre-specified unique marker sequences for
each species.

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:39.790
So
for this particular tool called StrainPhlAn,

00:45:39.790 --> 00:45:43.160
we take a collection of reference sequence
information.

00:45:43.160 --> 00:45:47.240
We identify the
most unique sequences for each species.

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:52.210
And then given a new method genome, we can
map those short reads

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:59.279
to just these unique markers and cull snippet
variants within those constantly identified

00:45:59.279 --> 00:46:00.279
markers.

00:46:00.279 --> 00:46:06.920
This allows for each species and each sample
the construction of a strain level haplotype,

00:46:06.920 --> 00:46:13.540
a set of variants unique
to the strain of that species in each sample.

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:16.480
And these can then be visualized in a number
of different ways, some

00:46:16.480 --> 00:46:20.200
of which are exactly the same as you would
do for microbial genetics in a collection

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:24.960
of isolates by looking at the
phylogeny, or divergence, between strains

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:27.180
in different samples.

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:29.220
You can associate these with phenotype.

00:46:29.220 --> 00:46:35.140
So for example, this is the phylogeny of the
strains of one organism across many different

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:39.589
gut microbiomes, this
set of strains is enriched for the cartoon

00:46:39.589 --> 00:46:43.320
red phenotype, and this set of strains is
enriched for the purple

00:46:43.320 --> 00:46:44.440
phenotype.

00:46:44.440 --> 00:46:49.270
Equivalently, you can show the same type of
information, again, in an ordination like

00:46:49.270 --> 00:46:50.470
we saw earlier.

00:46:50.470 --> 00:46:57.339
But here each point represents the strain
of a particular species in one metagenome.

00:46:57.339 --> 00:47:01.109
And distance between
points represents how diverged, genetically,

00:47:01.109 --> 00:47:02.279
those strains are.

00:47:02.279 --> 00:47:08.109
So using that, we can both associate strains
with phenotypes, like I showed in the previous

00:47:08.109 --> 00:47:11.890
slide, or ask some
basic biological questions about how strains

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:15.039
evolve in the human microbiome in the first
place.

00:47:15.039 --> 00:47:18.940
So one of the ways
in which we did this was by comparing the

00:47:18.940 --> 00:47:24.569
genetic diversity of organisms within each
body site to the diversity

00:47:24.569 --> 00:47:27.019
between body sites.

00:47:27.019 --> 00:47:32.190
Some organisms like to specialize to a particular
biochemical environment, and essentially

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:35.890
diverge into different organisms across body
sites.

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:38.710
Whereas other organisms transfer genetic material
more

00:47:38.710 --> 00:47:41.809
freely, and are more cosmopolitan.

00:47:41.809 --> 00:47:43.700
And you can see this better with some examples.

00:47:43.700 --> 00:47:49.180
Where if I take a point that's right on this
diagonal, the diversity

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:51.690
of strep salivarius.

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:54.980
Between and within body sites is roughly the
same.

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:59.390
So each point here represents one strain of
strep salivarius from a number of different

00:47:59.390 --> 00:48:00.480
body sites.

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:04.250
You can see there's a great deal of genetic
diversity, but it doesn't really segregate

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:06.470
within any particular part of the
oral cavity.

00:48:06.470 --> 00:48:09.990
And so this is a typically oral microbe.

00:48:09.990 --> 00:48:15.900
A counterexample is something like haemophilus
parainfluenzae, which is off the diagonal

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:16.900
here.

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:21.180
This organism has a comparable amount of genetic
diversity, but

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:25.539
it's started to differentiate into groups
of strains that are unique to each of the

00:48:25.539 --> 00:48:27.329
three main oral sites.

00:48:27.329 --> 00:48:31.289
So you can
actually find different types of h.parainfluenze

00:48:31.289 --> 00:48:34.810
on the tooth surface, versus the cheek, versus
the tongue.

00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:40.400
This is the level of biochemical specialization
that some microbes in the microbiome are able

00:48:40.400 --> 00:48:41.400
to attain.

00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:44.579
And you
can actually differentiate different ecological

00:48:44.579 --> 00:48:49.970
strategies genetically across very, very nearby
surfaces within the

00:48:49.970 --> 00:48:51.420
oral cavity.

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:57.029
So, finally, I'll wrap up now we have methods
for detecting who is there in the microbiome,

00:48:57.029 --> 00:49:02.049
but with a little bit about
functional profiling and understanding the

00:49:02.049 --> 00:49:04.819
biochemistry that we can detect from metagenomes
and

00:49:04.819 --> 00:49:06.630
metatranscriptomes.

00:49:06.630 --> 00:49:14.680
So a quick one slide summary of one of the
methods we have to do that starts with, again,

00:49:14.680 --> 00:49:17.730
a
metagenome or metatranscriptome.

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:22.210
And after detecting which organisms are present
in the sample using the

00:49:22.210 --> 00:49:28.900
methods they described earlier, retrieve their
reference sequences from an underlying database.

00:49:28.900 --> 00:49:35.289
So not just the unique markers or the most
specific sequences, but all genes or the pan-genome

00:49:35.289 --> 00:49:38.980
associated with
each organism in a community.

00:49:38.980 --> 00:49:45.059
Maps, metagenomic or metatranscriptomic reads
to those genes, count up hits,

00:49:45.059 --> 00:49:51.339
and can thereby describe which genes and which
organisms are carrying them in a community.

00:49:51.339 --> 00:49:56.280
And then, finally,
for reasons that do not map to specific nucleotides,

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:59.410
we carry out a more computationally expensive
translated

00:49:59.410 --> 00:50:00.770
search.

00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:04.930
Hits from this process to a larger protein
database can still tell you something about

00:50:04.930 --> 00:50:08.559
the biochemistry of
what that gene might be doing, but not necessarily

00:50:08.559 --> 00:50:10.700
which organism is churning it.

00:50:10.700 --> 00:50:16.740
So this gives a functional profile of a microbial
community in which we can quantify-- very

00:50:16.740 --> 00:50:21.510
much like you would for
say a transfer [INAUDIBLE] or RNA seek-- the

00:50:21.510 --> 00:50:25.200
abundance of each gene family in the community
in units that are

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:29.750
basically like RPKMS or relative abundances.

00:50:29.750 --> 00:50:32.680
And these are broken down, when possible,
into the contributing

00:50:32.680 --> 00:50:38.670
organism for each gene family, or into a set
of unclassified organisms when we only know

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:41.820
that the protein level if
the gene family is there.

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:47.079
For the subset of these gene families that
are biochemically characterized, we can also

00:50:47.079 --> 00:50:52.480
put them back together into pathways and do
the same thing to quantify and taxonomically

00:50:52.480 --> 00:50:56.089
annotate the
contributions of each pathway to a community,

00:50:56.089 --> 00:50:58.569
in addition to the uncharacterized gene families.

00:50:58.569 --> 00:51:01.329
So I'm running a little low on time.

00:51:01.329 --> 00:51:04.561
I'll move quickly through the last few examples
of using this within the human

00:51:04.561 --> 00:51:11.020
microbiome, including things like finding
human specific pathways, so pathways that

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:16.099
are carried microbially across
many different biochemical environments on

00:51:16.099 --> 00:51:17.309
the body.

00:51:17.309 --> 00:51:20.040
And these speak to processes these that are
enriched in

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:21.640
host associated microbes.

00:51:21.640 --> 00:51:27.150
So things that bugs have to do somewhere in
their ecology in order to survive in

00:51:27.150 --> 00:51:29.920
association with a human being.

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:35.249
And you can see from this one example of vitamin
D metabolism on the bottom here, that across

00:51:35.249 --> 00:51:39.880
the body many
different organisms might carry out this process.

00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:42.910
But it seems to be necessary for it to be
carried out by some

00:51:42.910 --> 00:51:46.180
organism within the community to form a stable
ecology.

00:51:46.180 --> 00:51:50.499
And this is in addition, of course, to body
site or

00:51:50.499 --> 00:51:55.960
biochemically specific processes that are
carried out by different bugs within a specific

00:51:55.960 --> 00:51:56.960
environment.

00:51:56.960 --> 00:52:02.180
So, for, example nitrate reduction is always
carried out by some organism in the oral cavity.

00:52:02.180 --> 00:52:05.660
But this can, again, be
a different organism from person to person

00:52:05.660 --> 00:52:08.180
or mouth to mouth, in this case.

00:52:08.180 --> 00:52:13.690
So this type of functional profiling
gives us sort of a way of linking ecology

00:52:13.690 --> 00:52:18.609
and biochemistry in the microbiome by looking
at the diversity of

00:52:18.609 --> 00:52:25.829
organisms that can provide a pathway, both
within an individual or between individuals.

00:52:25.829 --> 00:52:30.799
Ranging from pathways that are more or less
core to the human microbiome-- they must be

00:52:30.799 --> 00:52:34.559
present in every
individual in some environments.

00:52:34.559 --> 00:52:39.259
Sometimes, rarely, these are provided by a
specific organism.

00:52:39.259 --> 00:52:42.849
And that organism
is the same between individuals.

00:52:42.849 --> 00:52:47.029
But more commonly, there's diversity in which
organisms can provide a particular

00:52:47.029 --> 00:52:48.680
type of biochemistry.

00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:54.670
So here's an example in which only one organism
per individual tends to provide a pathway,

00:52:54.670 --> 00:52:58.519
but that organism
can differ between individuals.

00:52:58.519 --> 00:53:02.980
And then, finally, more typically there can
be many different microbes per individual

00:53:02.980 --> 00:53:08.359
that can carry out a particular type of biochemistry,
and often it's not even the same bugs per

00:53:08.359 --> 00:53:09.519
individual.

00:53:09.519 --> 00:53:13.700
So there
can be diversity both in which organisms per

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:18.869
individual provide a pathway, and in how they
change between

00:53:18.869 --> 00:53:19.869
individuals.

00:53:19.869 --> 00:53:27.509
So this leads us to some of the challenges
in the microbial community and human microbiome

00:53:27.509 --> 00:53:30.430
analysis and data
science, which range all the way from the

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:34.500
very molecular focus that I was just describing
up to higher level

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:38.799
questions of population health and microbial
interaction.

00:53:38.799 --> 00:53:41.859
I highlighted already a lot of the dark matter
or open

00:53:41.859 --> 00:53:46.910
questions around the underlying biochemistry
or cellular biology that we can see in the

00:53:46.910 --> 00:53:49.069
microbiome from these
different types of molecular data.

00:53:49.069 --> 00:53:52.150
And, of course, many of these affect translation
in human health.

00:53:52.150 --> 00:53:57.480
As I mentioned in the beginning, the microbiome
provides not just a high dimensional read

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:00.750
out of human health,
but also a potential point of intervention.

00:54:00.750 --> 00:54:04.420
Once we know the right direction in which
to push it, we can make

00:54:04.420 --> 00:54:08.869
changes to a microbial community in a way
that it would be very difficult with human

00:54:08.869 --> 00:54:14.609
genetics And, finally, many of
these human considerations have effects at

00:54:14.609 --> 00:54:16.349
the population level as well.

00:54:16.349 --> 00:54:23.130
So, for example, early life anti-microbial
or probiotic exposures might then influence

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:28.190
microbiome acquisition or development, and
thus later life allergic or

00:54:28.190 --> 00:54:30.400
atopic phenotypes as well.

00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:36.420
So all the tools that I briefly mentioned
today are available online as part of the

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:42.559
labs bioBakery collection, both of
individual open source methods, and a virtual

00:54:42.559 --> 00:54:45.619
image that includes all of these pre-built
and documented and set

00:54:45.619 --> 00:54:47.900
up within a single environment.

00:54:47.900 --> 00:54:52.390
So if you're interested in follow up work
using any of these methods, take a look at

00:54:52.390 --> 00:54:55.210
the bioBakery site.

00:54:55.210 --> 00:54:59.390
And I'd like to thank all of those faces that
you saw going by who are the people who actually

00:54:59.390 --> 00:55:04.650
did the work, either in the lab-- or several
of our collaborators much of his work was

00:55:04.650 --> 00:55:07.210
with Ramnik Xavier, at the
Broad.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:13.609
I mentioned Owen and [INAUDIBLE], group at
the University of Maryland.

00:55:13.609 --> 00:55:17.019
Wendy Garrett here at School of Public
Health.

00:55:17.019 --> 00:55:20.819
And then several former lab members such as
Nicholas [INAUDIBLE], who have since gone

00:55:20.819 --> 00:55:26.079
on to carry on
a great deal of this work on their own.

00:55:26.079 --> 00:55:27.710
So with that, I'll wrap up.

00:55:27.710 --> 00:55:29.800
Again, thanks to the organizers, the audience.

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:32.849
And
I think there is a little time left for questions.

00:55:32.849 --> 00:55:36.349
Curtis, this was a fantastic talk.

00:55:36.349 --> 00:55:37.989
Thank you so much for sharing this with us.

00:55:37.989 --> 00:55:43.160
This is really-- I've got about 4,000
questions, and in five minutes to ask them

00:55:43.160 --> 00:55:44.160
in.

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:47.789
But I want to-- just these are some questions
of my own.

00:55:47.789 --> 00:55:52.880
And as
somebody who's not a microbiomisist, this

00:55:52.880 --> 00:55:54.280
is fascinating for me.

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:59.339
And when I-- back in the olden days when I
was learning about genetics, there was a real

00:55:59.339 --> 00:56:05.950
emphasis-- at least kind
of during the initial phases of the microbiological

00:56:05.950 --> 00:56:12.560
genomics kind of explosion-- there was still
a lot of interest in

00:56:12.560 --> 00:56:21.680
doing population genetics, and looking at
things like gene fitness and as genes propagate

00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:23.240
through a distribution
and whatnot.

00:56:23.240 --> 00:56:30.440
Is there any sort of, in your opinion, opportunities
for those old school population geneticist

00:56:30.440 --> 00:56:35.079
mathematicians to take a look at some of these
data and look at gene frequencies in some

00:56:35.079 --> 00:56:39.280
of these different
populations, and what happens to those genes

00:56:39.280 --> 00:56:42.369
as they spread through populations and either
are beneficial or

00:56:42.369 --> 00:56:43.369
otherwise?

00:56:43.369 --> 00:56:44.999
Does that make sense?

00:56:44.999 --> 00:56:46.589
That makes sense.

00:56:46.589 --> 00:56:50.130
It's a great question and very timely.

00:56:50.130 --> 00:56:51.819
One of the things that I think has been exciting
about the

00:56:51.819 --> 00:56:58.339
past years worth of computational biology
in the microbiome is the ability to see that

00:56:58.339 --> 00:57:03.380
kind of data at sufficient
resolution to do sort of classical microbial

00:57:03.380 --> 00:57:07.109
genetics in a culture independent or metagenomic
way.

00:57:07.109 --> 00:57:10.749
So some of
these strain identification methods, for example,

00:57:10.749 --> 00:57:14.720
have only just recently become precise enough
to track strain

00:57:14.720 --> 00:57:21.720
variation and divergence in population genetics
for every bug in a community at the same time.

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:26.400
And we're starting
to get to the ten thousands of gut microbiome

00:57:26.400 --> 00:57:30.910
samples available globally to do that sort
of tracking across multiple

00:57:30.910 --> 00:57:32.359
populations simultaneously.

00:57:32.359 --> 00:57:37.930
So I think--
Yeah, I think that is-- yeah, that is so--

00:57:37.930 --> 00:57:38.930
it's so exciting.

00:57:38.930 --> 00:57:41.059
Because, you know, when you're talking about
population

00:57:41.059 --> 00:57:48.859
genetics at least, you know, the Cavalli-Sforza
and Bodmer type of old school population genetics,

00:57:48.859 --> 00:57:51.359
you know, that
was all like very theoretical, right?

00:57:51.359 --> 00:57:55.269
Lots of differential equations to try to theoretically
analyze what would happen if

00:57:55.269 --> 00:58:00.599
a gene spread through a population to fixation
and Hady-Weinberg equilibrium's and so forth

00:58:00.599 --> 00:58:01.749
and so on.

00:58:01.749 --> 00:58:05.220
You
actually have the population of bugs to analyze

00:58:05.220 --> 00:58:08.950
and watch what happens as a new gene gets
introduced into that

00:58:08.950 --> 00:58:11.519
population and what happens to it.

00:58:11.519 --> 00:58:16.470
And be able to watch the behavior of the bugs,
watch their biochemical output

00:58:16.470 --> 00:58:21.229
as a consequence of as these genes spread
through that population what happens to them.

00:58:21.229 --> 00:58:22.770
And is that beneficial
or not?

00:58:22.770 --> 00:58:27.930
And I was also thinking about something you
had mentioned about the specificity of the

00:58:27.930 --> 00:58:30.309
different bugs for their
various niches on the body.

00:58:30.309 --> 00:58:35.722
Where you have, say, something that lives
on the skin is not necessarily the same

00:58:35.722 --> 00:58:38.319
population of bugs which live inside the gut.

00:58:38.319 --> 00:58:42.849
And I was also curious about-- because it
looked like you were sort of

00:58:42.849 --> 00:58:47.440
talking about-- I don't know if you touched
on it in, particular but immune responses

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:51.220
to when, say, something that's
even from an individual.

00:58:51.220 --> 00:58:57.549
Something like bugs from their gut get introduced
accidentally into a different area of the

00:58:57.549 --> 00:58:58.549
body.

00:58:58.549 --> 00:59:01.779
So I was thinking in my head, like, what happens
when-- because for those of us who've had

00:59:01.779 --> 00:59:05.559
kids-- what happens
when bugs from the gut get introduced into

00:59:05.559 --> 00:59:06.769
the eye.

00:59:06.769 --> 00:59:08.729
This is basically pinkeye, right?

00:59:08.729 --> 00:59:13.930
So you basically get gut bugs
introduced into the conjunctiva of the eye,

00:59:13.930 --> 00:59:17.369
and you get an immune response of swelling
and pus and all sorts of

00:59:17.369 --> 00:59:18.829
other nastiness.

00:59:18.829 --> 00:59:22.259
And there's a really dramatic and obvious
immune response.

00:59:22.259 --> 00:59:27.279
Can you imagine being able to look
at the role of the immune system in all of

00:59:27.279 --> 00:59:28.279
this?

00:59:28.279 --> 00:59:29.279
Definitely.

00:59:29.279 --> 00:59:34.579
It's much more I should say Wendy's area of
expertise than mine.

00:59:34.579 --> 00:59:40.710
But we work with her and Ramnik on
immune interactions quite a bit.

00:59:40.710 --> 00:59:46.009
In the gut-- the kind of example that you
raised comes up in the context of gut

00:59:46.009 --> 00:59:53.720
barrier leakiness, and the degree to which,
again, in a sort of cyclical way post triggered

00:59:53.720 --> 01:00:00.710
inflammation can allow
microbial penetration through the gut.

01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:07.289
And then conversely, microbial invasion can
trigger inflammation and

01:00:07.289 --> 01:00:10.019
sustain that leakiness.

01:00:10.019 --> 01:00:13.359
So that certainly is a factor in inflammatory
bowel disease.

01:00:13.359 --> 01:00:15.470
Systemically there is interest.

01:00:15.470 --> 01:00:20.809
And we've worked with
folks like Dan Libman on rheumatoid arthritis.

01:00:20.809 --> 01:00:25.779
Where certainly either leakiness or perturbation
in the gut or

01:00:25.779 --> 01:00:32.869
potentially circulation or triggers from distal
microbes-- like oral microbes-- could play

01:00:32.869 --> 01:00:38.160
a factor in sort of mistriggering
the immune system systemically, and then having

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:42.329
it target things like the synovium at the
joints in

01:00:42.329 --> 01:00:43.759
rheumatoid arthritis.

01:00:43.759 --> 01:00:45.200
That's really fascinating.

01:00:45.200 --> 01:00:53.849
And one final question is, can you give us
a comment on microbial transplantation, and

01:00:53.849 --> 01:00:57.309
what sort of horizons exist for that?

01:00:57.309 --> 01:00:59.359
Well, it's a great question.

01:00:59.359 --> 01:01:05.460
And, you know, every everyone likes to talk
about foot transplants at the end of the talk.

01:01:05.460 --> 01:01:06.460
Exactly.

01:01:06.460 --> 01:01:13.460
So there's-- I would certainly put that pico
microbiome transplants in-- one of the real

01:01:13.460 --> 01:01:18.549
translational success stories
of that-- the microbiome so far-- certainly

01:01:18.549 --> 01:01:24.190
for Clostridium difficile there's been a lot
of great results in properly

01:01:24.190 --> 01:01:30.349
screened and clinically controlled transplants
being able to really ameliorate recurrent

01:01:30.349 --> 01:01:31.349
c-diff.

01:01:31.349 --> 01:01:35.349
And there's starting to
be more work in FMTs for inflammatory bowel

01:01:35.349 --> 01:01:38.920
disease, where the ecology is harder to restore.

01:01:38.920 --> 01:01:42.739
Again, we don't
necessarily know yet what the right push is

01:01:42.739 --> 01:01:47.580
to get the community to get it back to stability
within an individual.

01:01:47.580 --> 01:01:50.769
And
there's a lot of subjective subject variation.

01:01:50.769 --> 01:01:55.589
So I think some of the neatest work going
on in that space right now is in understanding,

01:01:55.589 --> 01:02:00.700
one, what is the best
push to give to, say, an ulcerative colitis

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:01.700
individual?

01:02:01.700 --> 01:02:04.249
What's the right group of microbes?

01:02:04.249 --> 01:02:07.390
Whether that should
come from an FMT, or whether you can make

01:02:07.390 --> 01:02:11.530
an artificial cocktail that behaves like an
FMT.

01:02:11.530 --> 01:02:15.029
And then, two, how to tailor that to each
individual.

01:02:15.029 --> 01:02:17.880
So that no matter what their sort of current
or baseline

01:02:17.880 --> 01:02:22.309
microbiome is, that push gets them back to
a healthy state.

01:02:22.309 --> 01:02:24.289
And where things like, again, strain tracking
and

01:02:24.289 --> 01:02:28.890
understanding the biochemistry or functional
profile of what these bugs should be doing

01:02:28.890 --> 01:02:31.930
is helpful.

01:02:31.930 --> 01:02:38.810
Well, with that I'd like to thank Curtis Huttenhower
from Harvard Medical School-- or School of

01:02:38.810 --> 01:02:42.569
Public Health--
excuse me-- for his fantastic talk.

01:02:42.569 --> 01:02:45.979
I'm just so just blown away.

01:02:45.979 --> 01:02:50.430
I have still another several thousand questions,
but

01:02:50.430 --> 01:02:52.190
we've run out of time.

01:02:52.190 --> 01:02:53.190
But thank you, Curtis.

01:02:53.190 --> 01:02:54.190
That was really fantastic.

01:02:54.190 --> 01:02:56.410
And thanks to everyone who joined us today.

